design.attest:
Attested demand, all of these:
- at least 3 evidence items (each a public https URL, its host, a date and what it says in 25 words or fewer);
- from at least 2 distinct hosts;
- of at least 2 distinct kinds (looking, paying for a workaround, complaining), or at least 1 'paying';
- every item dated within 365 days of the scan and not in the future.
The same URL counts once. Anything less stays a daydream: logged, never tested.

design.test:
The cheapest demand test, written before it runs. Its fields:
- kind: post, issue, listing, landing or waitlist;
- channel;
- the metric it counts: clicks, signups, reactions, installs or preorders;
- the bar: a whole number of at least 1;
- the exposure that makes it a real test: a metric and a minimum;
- the window: open and close dates, at most 30 days;
- its cost in pounds;
- the date it was sealed, on or before it opens.
A test that takes money, sets a price, or collects personal data (a waitlist, a pre-sell, a buy button) is refused unless it records Simon's approval.

design.judge:
The test's verdict, from its sealed numbers alone:
- OPEN before it closes;
- UNRUN when the exposure fell short of the sealed minimum (never a kill: nobody saw it);
- BUILD when the metric reached the bar;
- KILL otherwise.
